#{[17592186540738 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540763 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540766 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540781 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540854 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540855 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540869 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540871 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540877 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186540893 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540904 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186540919 :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186541057 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{2}(t)-4,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186541057 :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-2}\\}-{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2\\cdot t}-{e}^{4\\cdot t}+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\cdot t}+1 \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{2t}+1 \\\\ {e}^{4t}+1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541069 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.$"] [17592186541079 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186541079 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541095 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P.$"] [17592186541095 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$ Computing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{array}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|c}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186541107 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{7} & \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7} & \\,∗\\, & \\frac{3}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} & \\,∗\\, & -\\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186541107 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{6}{7} \\\\ \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has both properties."] [17592186541114 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}$ ."] [17592186541124 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186541124 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541136 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$"] [17592186541136 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find  eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms, to obtain the first and second columns of $P:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541149 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"190px\">$2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186541149 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541281 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2 x + y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(2 x + y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(u v\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(u v\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{2}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\left(2 u\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 u\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[u^{2}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541290 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(3\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{6}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{12}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541298 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x - y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x - y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u v}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{9}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(6\\right)}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{u}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{6}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541305 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 2\\le w\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)^{2} \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u^{2} v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{2}^{6}\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v w}{6}\\right]_{w=2}^{w=6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(6\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v \\left(2\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(u^{2} v\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2 u^{2} v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u^{2} v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2} \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{4 u^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(u^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{3}}{3}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541321 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 3 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le v\\le 4,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 3 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{9}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v w \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(\\frac{v \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{4}\\left(\\frac{2 v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(4\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{16 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[5 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(5\\right)-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541328 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\quad v=y + 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 3,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(3 x - 2 y\\right) \\left(y + 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|\\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\int_{0}^{4}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(4\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{3}\\left(\\frac{2 u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{3}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(3\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{3}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(3 u\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 u\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3 u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541332 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=- 3 x + 2 y,\\quad v=y - 2 z,\\quad w=2 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):0\\le u\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le v\\le 1,\\quad 0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(2\\right)\\left(\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(- 3 x + 2 y\\right) \\left(y - 2 z\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u v\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{6}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{u v}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v w}{6}\\right]_{w=0}^{w=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(2\\right)}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u v \\left(0\\right)}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u v}{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u v^{2}}{6}\\right]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{6}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{u}{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{12}\\right]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{12}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541334 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le v\\le 2 \\pi,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x^{2} + 4 y\\right) \\cos{\\left(x + 2 z \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{12}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u w \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(\\frac{u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{v=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{v=2 \\pi}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(0\\right)-\\left(\\frac{u}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- \\frac{u}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[- \\frac{u^{2}}{8}\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541338 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x^{2} + 4 y,\\quad v=x + 2 z,\\quad w=3 z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 1\\le v\\le 2,\\quad 3\\le w\\le 9\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(3\\right)\\left(\\left(2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=- \\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(\\left(x + 2 z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x^{2} + 4 y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left|- \\frac{1}{12}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{3}^{9}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v w \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right]_{w=3}^{w=9}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{v \\left(9\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\left(3\\right) \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{12}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(\\frac{v \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left[\\frac{v^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\left(\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(\\frac{3 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{u=\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{u=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) \\right)}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541340 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - \\frac{y}{4},\\quad v=\\frac{y}{4},\\quad w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v,w):1\\le u\\le 3,\\quad 1\\le v\\le 3,\\quad \\frac{1}{2}\\le w\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial v}{\\partial z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} & \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y,z)}{\\partial(u,v,w)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v,w)}{\\partial(x,y,z)}\\right)^{-1}=8.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\left(x - \\frac{y}{4} + \\frac{z}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}V & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(u + w\\right)\\cdot \\left|8\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\iiint_\\Delta \\left(8 u + 8 w\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\mathrm{d}v\\mathrm{d}w \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{1}\\left(8 u + 8 w\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}w\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[8 u w + 4 w^{2}\\right]_{w=\\frac{1}{2}}^{w=1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(4 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(4 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right)+\\left(8 u \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(8 u \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(4\\right)-\\left(1\\right)+\\left(8 u\\right)-\\left(4 u\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(4 u + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left[v \\left(4 u + 3\\right)\\right]_{v=1}^{v=3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(3 \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right)\\right)+\\left(4 u \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(4 u \\left(1\\right)\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(\\left(9\\right)-\\left(3\\right)+\\left(12 u\\right)-\\left(4 u\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(8 u + 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  & =\\left[4 u^{2} + 6 u\\right]_{u=1}^{u=3} \\\\  & =\\left(4 \\left(3\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(4 \\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right)+\\left(6 \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(6 \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(36\\right)-\\left(4\\right)+\\left(18\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186541523 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -9 \\\\ 36 & 36\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541532 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541555 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541576 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541589 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541593 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{8t}"] [17592186541629 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541638 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541656 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541658 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 70 & 140 \\\\ 0 & 80 & 160\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541661 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541713 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 70 & 80 \\\\ 140 & 160\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541721 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541726 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541737 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541748 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541751 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 70 \\\\ 20 & 80\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541762 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541763 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541781 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541815 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541838 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541862 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541866 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541875 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541876 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541880 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541885 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -9 & 36 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 36\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541918 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186541932 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186542139 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(4Ax-5b)^{T}(4Ax-5b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$832$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186542139 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=4Ax-5b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=4Ax-5b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=4{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-5{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=4{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 4{x}_{j} \\\\  & =8{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $8$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=8y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =8y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4Ax-5b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8(\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}208 \\\\ -248\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 832 \\\\ 0 & -992\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186542179 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186542179 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186542190 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186542190 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0\\end{bmatrix})=2(\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186542191 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186542220 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*"] [17592186542220 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186542227 :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186542714 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2} & {u}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186542768 :answer/value "{X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186542911 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186543139 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2} & {u}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186543214 :answer/value "{X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186543284 :answer/value "{\\begin{bmatrix}{u}_{1} & {u}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\cdot {X}_{\\text{std}}"] [17592186543481 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & -0.95 & -1.02 \\\\ 0.79 & 0.33 & 1.56 \\\\ -1.48 & -1.17 & -0.66 \\\\ 0.67 & 0.8 & -0.15 \\\\ 0.63 & 0.99 & 0.27\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.462 & 0.174 & -0.685 & 0.1 & 0.527 \\\\ 0.473 & -0.718 & -0.24 & 0.358 & 0.272 \\\\ -0.613 & -0.29 & 0.546 & 0.457 & 0.181 \\\\ 0.253 & 0.517 & -0.053 & 0.798 & -0.17 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.319 & 0.416 & -0.127 & 0.766\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.171 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.477 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.615 & 0.231 & -0.754 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.505 & 0.633 \\\\ 0.527 & -0.832 & 0.175\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186543482 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $8\\times 6$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has one negative singular value."] [17592186543511 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has one negative singular value.\n\nII. Any $2\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values."] [17592186543515 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.93 & 0.98 & -1.44 & -1.13 \\\\ -0.14 & -0.96 & 0.96 & 1.58 \\\\ -1.06 & -1.09 & -0.61 & -0.05 \\\\ -0.86 & 0.96 & 0.42 & 0.08 \\\\ 1.12 & 0.1 & 0.68 & -0.48\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.725 & 0.1 & -0.166 & -0.483 & 0.452 \\\\ 0.64 & -0.269 & -0.185 & -0.532 & 0.449 \\\\ 0.207 & 0.729 & -0.311 & 0.363 & 0.444 \\\\ 0.032 & 0.06 & 0.889 & 0.076 & 0.446 \\\\ -0.149 & -0.619 & -0.227 & 0.588 & 0.444\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.067 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.933 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.517 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.763 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.384 & -0.717 & -0.539 & -0.218 \\\\ -0.5 & -0.229 & 0.78 & -0.297 \\\\ 0.471 & -0.643 & 0.31 & 0.518 \\\\ 0.617 & -0.141 & 0.06 & -0.772\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186543563 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.795 & -5.044 & 1.480 \\\\ 2.471 & -4.893 & 1.132 \\\\ 2.589 & -6.272 & -0.161 \\\\ 0.548 & -6.608 & 1.020 \\\\ 5.379 & -3.807 & 3.126\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.81 & 0.25 & 0.14 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.38 & -0.16 \\\\ 0.12 & -0.84 & -1.25 \\\\ -0.94 & -1.13 & -0.25 \\\\ 1.57 & 1.34 & 1.53\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.066 & 0.590 & -0.424 & -0.067 & 0.680 \\\\ 0.055 & -0.056 & -0.505 & 0.841 & -0.178 \\\\ -0.374 & -0.731 & -0.063 & 0.057 & 0.565 \\\\ -0.430 & 0.317 & 0.675 & 0.487 & 0.152 \\\\ 0.817 & -0.116 & 0.325 & 0.220 & 0.405\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.124 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.309 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.738 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.544 & -0.802 & 0.248 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.173 & -0.775 \\\\ 0.578 & 0.572 & 0.581\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$"] [17592186543563 :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.81 & 0.25 & 0.14 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.38 & -0.16 \\\\ 0.12 & -0.84 & -1.25 \\\\ -0.94 & -1.13 & -0.25 \\\\ 1.57 & 1.34 & 1.53\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.066 & 0.590 & -0.424 & -0.067 & 0.680 \\\\ 0.055 & -0.056 & -0.505 & 0.841 & -0.178 \\\\ -0.374 & -0.731 & -0.063 & 0.057 & 0.565 \\\\ -0.430 & 0.317 & 0.675 & 0.487 & 0.152 \\\\ 0.817 & -0.116 & 0.325 & 0.220 & 0.405\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.124 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.309 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.738 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.544 & -0.802 & 0.248 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.173 & -0.775 \\\\ 0.578 & 0.572 & 0.581\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.124}^{2}}{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}+{0.738}^{2}}≈81.2\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}}{{3.124}^{2}+{1.309}^{2}+{0.738}^{2}}≈95.5\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $81.2\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $95.5\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."] [17592186544100 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544156 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186544175 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186544257 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544318 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544330 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544390 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544396 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186544507 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}"]}
